CA-431091 msync CBT log mapping before unmapping it

bitmap_init() maps the whole CBT log file MAP_SHARED from offset 0, so the
48-byte cbt_log_metadata header shares page 0 with the first 4048 bytes of
the bitmap - the low ~2GiB of the disk. Any guest write in that range
dirties the page holding the consistency flag.

bitmap_free() then dropped the mapping without flushing it. On a shared file
SR that leaves the dirty pages queued in this host's page cache after
tap-ctl pause has returned. The SR master renames the log onto the snapshot
and rewrites the header (cbt-util set -f 1) in that window, and our later
writeback pushes the whole of page 0 back, reinstating the stale header and
silently discarding the master's update. SM sees the log as inconsistent on
the next writable attach, deletes it and clears cbt_enabled, which fails the
subsequent VDI.data_destroy with VDI_NO_CBT_METADATA and breaks the CBT
chain.

Only hosts other than the SR master are affected: when the tapdisk is
co-located with cbt-util the two share a page cache and the header update
survives.

msync(MS_SYNC) before munmap() completes our writeback before pause returns,
restoring the ordering SM already assumes.

diff --git a/drivers/block-log.c b/drivers/block-log.c
index 31481f5..a85aa8b 100644
--- a/drivers/block-log.c
+++ b/drivers/block-log.c
@@ -115,6 +115,17 @@ static int bitmap_free(struct tdlog_data *data)
 	int rc;
 	bmsize = bitmap_size(data->size * SECTOR_SIZE) + sizeof(struct cbt_log_metadata);
 	if (data->bitmap) {
+		/* The mapping covers the log header as well as the bitmap, and
+		 * writeback is per page, so page 0 carries both. On a shared file
+		 * SR the SR master stamps the header as soon as we are paused; if
+		 * our dirty pages are still queued then, the eventual writeback
+		 * reinstates the stale header and discards that update. Flush
+		 * synchronously so we are written back before pause completes. */
+		rc = msync(data->bitmap, bmsize, MS_SYNC);
+		if (rc != 0) {
+			EPRINTF("Failed to sync the bitmap block");
+		}
+
 		rc = munmap(data->bitmap, bmsize);
 		if (rc != 0) {
 			EPRINTF("Failed to unmap the bitmap block");
